---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Method — Tom Dimnet"
  description="How I design courses and train the trainers, with the work that proves it. Content, methodology, and the level almost nobody works on: posture."
  current="method"
>
  <div class="region">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <h1 class="page">Method</h1>
      <p class="altitude">
        How I design courses and train the trainers, with the work that proves
        it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <h2>Most "train the trainer" isn't training anyone.</h2>
        <p>
          Ask what it means and you'll get the same answer: take someone who
          knows the subject, walk them through the slides, maybe do a dry run.
          They're a trainer now.
        </p>
        <p>
          Call it what it is: content transfer. It's efficient, it scales, and
          it's where most organizations stop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">
        You haven't trained a trainer. You've briefed a narrator.
      </p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          One level up is methodology transfer. You stop handing over slides and
          start explaining the structure: why this exercise comes before that
          concept, why there's a silence at minute twelve, why assessment runs
          through the session instead of sitting at the end. Rarer, but it
          exists. It's what I'm building at Scaleway.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then there's a third level almost nobody works on in corporate
          training, though it's been central to teacher education in France for
          decades. Posture. Not what you do in a session — who you are when the
          session stops going to plan.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <h2>The level you can't hand over.</h2>
        <p>
          Here's the paradox. The trainers who read a room best are the ones
          least able to explain how they do it.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's a well-documented model of competence with four stages, ending
          in unconscious competence — you do the thing well without thinking
          about it. That's the goal everywhere else. In teaching, it's a trap:
          to transmit something, you first have to make it visible again, which
          means traveling back from stage four to stage two. You have to
          remember what it felt like not to know. It's disorienting, because the
          whole point of stage four is that you stopped paying attention.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take the most ordinary moment there is. You ask a question and the
          room goes quiet. The problem isn't the silence — it's that you don't
          know what it means. Are they thinking, or are they lost? Same silence,
          opposite meanings, opposite responses, and you have about two seconds.
          Push, or wait. Rephrase, or say nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing in the content or the plan tells you which. Experienced
          trainers get it right most of the time and can rarely say why.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I've found so far is that it starts with naming the invisible —
          putting words on decisions that were never conscious, so they can be
          discussed at all.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">
        It's the part of the work I spend the most time on and understand the
        least.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <h2>What it looks like when it's done.</h2>
        <p>
          A school hired me to teach teenagers to program. What they actually
          needed was someone to rebuild the curriculum — three years of material
          with no real progression, not across a year and not across the three.
          Topics showed up because they existed, not because anything before
          them made them necessary.
        </p>
        <p>
          I spent the first year rebuilding it from zero: deciding what to
          teach, in what order, and what to leave out entirely. The second year,
          with the progression in place, it held.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">The same act as designing one session — one scale up.</p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          And it's the work I'm doing now at Scaleway, one scale up again —
          building the program that teaches engineers to teach, and designing
          the trainings myself so there's something to point at.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dry">
      <p class="dry-kicker">Selected courses</p>
      <p class="dry-intro">
        Eight courses for OpenClassrooms, two for NX Academy. Here are three.
      </p>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>Containerize Your Applications with Docker</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          NX Academy · French · <a href="https://nx.academy/"
            >see the course ↗</a
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          Everything a Docker course is supposed to contain is in there. Almost
          none of it is in the first hour. Networks show up three weeks later,
          the day you hit a problem they solve.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>Master CI/CD Pipelines with GitHub Actions</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          NX Academy · French · <a href="https://nx.academy/"
            >see the course ↗</a
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          One project runs through the entire course and is live by chapter two.
          Nothing gets introduced that the project doesn't need yet.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>Make Your Web Content Accessible</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          OpenClassrooms · English · <a href="#">see the course ↗</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          WCAG is a specification, not a curriculum. Follow its order and you've
          written a compliance document. This one starts from the people
          affected; the criteria arrive as answers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <h2>The work.</h2>
        <p>Three shapes it usually takes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="shapes">
      <div class="shape">
        <span class="num">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Building the program</h3>
          <p>
            An organization has engineers who know their subject and train
            badly, and no one whose job it is to fix that. This is the Scaleway
            shape: standards for how content gets made, a way to say why a
            training works or doesn't, and enough structure that it survives the
            person who built it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shape">
        <span class="num">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Training the trainers</h3>
          <p>
            Working with engineers who already teach — on methodology first,
            then on posture. Slower than a workshop and the only version that
            changes anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shape">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Designing the course</h3>
          <p>
            Sometimes what's needed is just the course, done properly.
            Curriculum, sequence, exercises, assessment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="aside">
      <h3>Training reviews</h3>
      <p>
        The one I'd like to do more of: take a real training, go through it
        section by section, and say what works, what doesn't, and why — the way
        you'd review code. It's the fastest way I know to make the invisible
        part discussable, and it works whether or not I built the thing.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="close">
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          I do this full-time at Scaleway. Outside of it, I take on a small
          number of programs and courses through NX Academy — enough to keep the
          practice honest, not enough to become an agency.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .shapes {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
  .shapes .shape {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 30px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 32px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .shapes .shape:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .shapes .num {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(44px, 6vw, 66px);
    line-height: 0.82;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .shapes .shape h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .shapes .shape p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .shapes .shape {
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      gap: 18px;
    }
    .shapes .num {
      font-size: 38px;
    }
  }
</style>
